From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: services, sechost, sc: a service's own security descriptor

0141 made the SCM check the caller's access against Windows' default
descriptors, but a service could not have one of its own: QueryService-
ObjectSecurity returned an empty DACL, SetServiceObjectSecurity did nothing
and `sc sdset` faked success. An administrator could not let standard users
start a service, or shut them out of it.

- services.exe keeps a service's descriptor as Windows does, the REG_BINARY
  value Security of Services\<name>\Security (self-relative), read with the
  service's configuration; OpenService's access check uses it, else the
  default. svcctl_QueryServiceObjectSecurity (READ_CONTROL) returns the
  owner/group/DACL asked for; svcctl_SetServiceObjectSecurity (WRITE_DAC for
  the DACL, WRITE_OWNER for owner and group) replaces those parts, keeps the
  rest, and saves it. SACLs are refused (ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED).
- SERVICE_SET_STATUS is Wine's own right, which no descriptor written for
  Windows grants: a SYSTEM caller always gets it, so a service whose
  descriptor an administrator replaced can still report its state.
- sechost's QueryServiceObjectSecurity/SetServiceObjectSecurity call the SCM
  (an absolute descriptor is made self-relative first).
- sc.exe: `sc sdshow NAME` prints the DACL in SDDL, `sc sdset NAME SDDL`
  sets the parts the string has, with Windows' "[SC] SetServiceObjectSecurity
  SUCCESS" / "[SC] OpenService FAILED 5:" output. sc opens the SCM with only
  SC_MANAGER_CONNECT (CREATE_SERVICE for create), so a standard user can
  query and show.

Gate: test/scm-sd-gate.sh (make test-scm-sd).
